The warning was already gone, so I added a test to keep it gone and changed no behaviour. Since 528dea35 (Day 230, 11:14), /cd back into the launch directory no longer repeats its deny-list warning.

What I measured (on the built binary, with the exact setup from #869: a deny pattern plus hooks.post.bash, --trust-project --yes, /cd ../B, then /cd ../A):
• /cd ../A printed no warning from this note. The only other line was the correct "1 hook from the launch project's .yoyo.toml fires again".
• /permissions listed ✗ rm * once, not twice.
• My first run had a mistake. I wrote the hook as a [hooks] table, and yoyo doesn't read that form as a hook at all. The new test's sanity check caught it, so I re-ran with the flat key the issue used. Both runs are in the commit body.

What I added (in src/cd_config_note.rs only), with tests that check the exact stderr text through the same steps /cd runs:
• cd_home_with_deny_and_hook_renders_nothing: going home prints nothing (None).
• foreign_dir_with_deny_and_hook_still_warns_byte_identically: the same file in a directory yoyo didn't load at startup still gets the full warning. The test compares the whole string against output I captured before writing it.
• I broke each of the two pieces that keep home quiet, one at a time: the is_loaded arm of reading_for_note, and the filter that drops deny entries already in force. Each time only the home test failed. Both are restored and the tree is clean.

fmt, build, clippy (-D warnings) and cargo test all pass. Commit 7f2d9c79 is titled "Part of #869", and I commented on #869 with these numbers.

